# Citation metadata (Citation File Format 1.2.0 — https://citation-file-format.github.io)
#
# CFF 1.2.0 has no field for a supervisory role: `authors` is the only person
# list the schema accepts at the top level, and adding an `advisors` key would
# make the file fail validation and break GitHub's "Cite this repository"
# widget. The advisor is therefore named in `abstract` rather than listed as an
# author, which would misstate authorship.
cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  Assessing the CRAFT Prompt Engineering Framework: A Paired Benchmark Study
message: >-
  If you refer to this study, its data, or this application, please cite it
  using the metadata in this file.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Kiprop
    given-names: Peter
abstract: >-
  A within-task paired benchmark study testing whether prompts structured with
  the CRAFT framework (Context, Role, Actions, Format, Tone) outperform baseline
  prompts of equivalent intent. Fifty tasks across six professional domains were
  run under both prompt conditions on two frontier models (claude-sonnet-5 and
  gpt-5.5), producing 320 generations scored by two LLM judges under a blinded,
  family-neutral rotation for 640 evaluations. Against a pronounced ceiling
  (baseline 9.12/10 pooled), CRAFT produced a statistically significant small
  negative effect on claude-sonnet-5 (d_z = -0.42, p = 0.0028) and no effect on
  gpt-5.5 (d_z = -0.04, p = 0.55). The repository contains the benchmark
  application, the task registry, and the complete run and evaluation record.
  Research advised by Prof. Vlad Veksler.
keywords:
  - prompt engineering
  - CRAFT framework
  - large language models
  - LLM-as-judge
  - benchmark study
  - paired comparison
repository-code: https://github.com/pkiprop-afk/CRAFT-Summer
version: 0.1.0
# Date the Phase 1 run and evaluation record was completed.
date-released: "2026-08-22"
